  "result": "\"More agents always give better results\" isn't true, so neither five nor ten is the right starting point. Start with one agent and split only when you can name the problem a split solves.\n\nYou probably assumed that dividing work among specialists works like dividing it among people. Each extra agent adds model calls, which cost money, and every handoff can lose or garble information. With a small budget, that cost shows up directly.\n\nSplitting pays off in two cases: one agent's context gets overloaded, or the subtasks are independent and can run in parallel. For your assistant, one agent with three tools (manual search, calendar check, booking) is a sensible baseline. The only plausible second agent is one that reads long manuals, so the main agent doesn't carry them in its context.\n\nWhich of your three tasks do you think would overload one agent's context first?",
